<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Drawables named only from Dart strings.

  The resource shrinker walks Java, Kotlin and XML to decide what is
  reachable. It cannot read a Dart string, so a drawable referenced only
  from Flutter looks unused and is deleted from release builds — and
  only release builds, which is what makes this so hard to see.

  Two separate casualties here, both in the audio feature:

  * `ic_notification` — named as `androidNotificationIcon:
    'drawable/ic_notification'` in bootstrap.dart.
  * `audio_service_*` — audio_service's own transport icons, named by
    `MediaControl.play`/`pause`/`stop` as `androidIcon:
    'drawable/audio_service_play_arrow'` and friends. These live in the
    plugin, not this app, which makes them easy to forget entirely.

  Losing the transport icons is worse than losing the notification icon,
  because it fails *earlier* and louder-but-invisibly: the platform
  throws `IllegalArgumentException: You must specify an icon resource id
  to build a CustomAction` out of `AudioService.setState`, before the
  `if (!wasPlaying && playing) enterPlayingState()` branch that starts
  the foreground service. So playback works, the UI updates, and there
  is simply never a notification — with the exception going only to
  System.err, once per state broadcast.

  The wildcard is deliberate: adding a rewind or skip control later must
  not silently reintroduce this.
-->
<resources
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    tools:keep="@drawable/ic_notification,@drawable/audio_service_*" />
